>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">No Employer Match? No Problem.</h2>
  <p>
    One of the biggest financial challenges for freelancers is planning for retirement without an employer
    401(k) match. But here's what most freelancers don't realize: <strong>self-employed workers actually
    have access to better retirement options than most employees.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    With a Solo 401(k), you can contribute up to $69,000 per year (2024)—far more than the $23,000 employee
    limit. You're both the employee AND the employer, which means you get to make contributions in both capacities.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide breaks down every retirement option available to freelancers, helps you choose the right one,
    and shows you how to set it up.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Prioritize Retirement -->
  <h2 id="why-prioritize">Why Freelancers Must Prioritize Retirement</h2>
  <p>
    No one is looking out for your retirement but you. Without automatic payroll deductions and employer
    matches, it's easy to push retirement savings to "someday."
  </p>

  <h3>The Cost of Waiting</h3>
  <p>
    Compound growth rewards early starters exponentially:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Starting at 25:</strong> $500/month grows to ~$1.4 million by 65 (7% return)</li>
    <li><strong>Starting at 35:</strong> Same $500/month reaches only ~$650,000</li>
    <li><strong>Starting at 45:</strong> Same amount reaches ~$280,000</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every year you delay costs tens of thousands in future wealth.
  </p>

  <h3>Tax Advantages Today</h3>
  <p>
    Retirement contributions provide immediate tax benefits:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Traditional contributions reduce your taxable income dollar-for-dollar</li>
    <li>Self-employment tax is calculated before retirement contributions</li>
    <li>A $20,000 contribution in the 24% bracket saves $4,800 in taxes—this year</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For freelancers with variable income, this flexibility is powerful. High-income year? Max out
    contributions. Slower year? Contribute less.
  </p>

  <h3>No One Else Will Do It</h3>
  <p>
    Employees benefit from automatic enrollment, payroll deductions, and employer matches—nudges that
    make saving easy. Freelancers must be intentional:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Choose and open an account</li>
    <li>Calculate contribution limits</li>
    <li>Make transfers (or set up automation)</li>
    <li>Track deadlines</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    It's more work, but the payoff is worth it: maximum flexibility and potentially higher contribution limits.
  </p>

  <!-- Solo 401(k) -->
  <h2 id="solo-401k">Solo 401(k) Explained</h2>
  <p>
    The Solo 401(k)—also called Individual 401(k) or Self-Employed 401(k)—is the most powerful retirement
    option for freelancers.
  </p>

  <h3>Contribution Limits (2024)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Employee contribution:</strong> Up to $23,000 (plus $7,500 catch-up if 50+)</li>
    <li><strong>Employer contribution:</strong> Up to 25% of net self-employment income</li>
    <li><strong>Total maximum:</strong> $69,000 ($76,500 with catch-up)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Who It's For</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Self-employed individuals with no full-time employees (spouse can be employed)</li>
    <li>Freelancers earning $50,000+ who want maximum tax deferral</li>
    <li>Those who want both Traditional (pre-tax) and Roth (post-tax) options</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Highest contribution limits:</strong> More than any other self-employed option</li>
    <li><strong>Roth option available:</strong> Some plans allow after-tax Roth contributions</li>
    <li><strong>Loan provision:</strong> Some plans allow borrowing from your balance</li>
    <li><strong>Dual contributions:</strong> Employee + employer means more flexibility</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Considerations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Requires annual Form 5500-SF filing once balance exceeds $250,000</li>
    <li>More complex to set up than SEP IRA</li>
    <li>Employee contribution deadline is December 31 (not tax filing deadline)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Where to Open</h3>
  <p>
    Major brokerages offer free Solo 401(k) plans:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Fidelity:</strong> Free setup and maintenance, excellent investment options</li>
    <li><strong>Charles Schwab:</strong> Free, includes Roth option</li>
    <li><strong>Vanguard:</strong> Free for most funds, trusted name</li>
    <li><strong>E*TRADE:</strong> Good for active traders</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- SEP IRA -->
  <h2 id="sep-ira">SEP IRA Explained</h2>
  <p>
    The Simplified Employee Pension (SEP) IRA is easier to administer than a Solo 401(k) while still
    offering generous contribution limits.
  </p>

  <h3>Contribution Limits (2024)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Maximum:</strong> 25% of net self-employment income, up to $69,000</li>
    <li>No employee contribution component—all contributions are "employer" contributions</li>
    <li>No catch-up contributions for those 50+</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Who It's For</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Freelancers who want simplicity over maximum flexibility</li>
    <li>Those who also have employees (though this can be expensive—you must contribute same percentage for employees)
    </li>
    <li>Freelancers with variable income who want percentage-based contributions</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Simple setup:</strong> Can be opened and funded in minutes</li>
    <li><strong>No annual filing:</strong> No Form 5500 required</li>
    <li><strong>Contribution deadline:</strong> Tax filing deadline including extensions (October 15)</li>
    <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> No minimum contribution required</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Considerations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>No Roth option—all contributions are pre-tax</li>
    <li>Lower effective contribution limit at lower incomes (25% of income)</li>
    <li>If you have employees, you must contribute same percentage for them</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>SEP IRA vs. Solo 401(k)</h3>
  <p>
    At higher incomes, the Solo 401(k) allows larger contributions because of the employee component.
    For example, with $100,000 in net self-employment income:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>SEP IRA:</strong> ~$18,587 maximum (25% of adjusted income)</li>
    <li><strong>Solo 401(k):</strong> ~$41,587 maximum ($23,000 employee + ~$18,587 employer)</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- SIMPLE IRA -->
  <h2 id="simple-ira">SIMPLE IRA for Freelancers with Contractors</h2>
  <p>
    The Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees (SIMPLE) IRA is designed for small businesses with employees,
    including contractors you might hire.
  </p>

  <h3>Contribution Limits (2024)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Employee contribution:</strong> Up to $16,000 (plus $3,500 catch-up if 50+)</li>
    <li><strong>Employer match:</strong> Either 3% match or 2% non-elective contribution</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Who It's For</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Freelancers with 1-100 employees</li>
    <li>Those who want employee participation without complex administration</li>
    <li>Businesses that want to offer retirement benefits to attract talent</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Advantages</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Employees can contribute (unlike SEP where only employer contributes)</li>
    <li>Lower administrative burden than 401(k)</li>
    <li>Immediate vesting for employees</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Considerations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Lower contribution limits than Solo 401(k) or SEP</li>
    <li>25% penalty for withdrawals in first 2 years (vs. 10% for other accounts)</li>
    <li>Can't have another retirement plan at the same business</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For most solo freelancers, the Solo 401(k) or SEP IRA is a better choice. SIMPLE IRA makes sense
    when you have employees who want to participate.
  </p>

  <!-- Traditional & Roth IRA -->
  <h2 id="ira">Traditional and Roth IRA</h2>
  <p>
    IRAs are the simplest retirement accounts and make excellent supplements to employer-style plans.
  </p>

  <h3>Contribution Limits (2024)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Maximum:</strong> $7,000 ($8,000 if 50+)</li>
    <li>Combined limit across Traditional and Roth IRAs</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Traditional IRA</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tax benefit:</strong> Deductible contribution, taxable withdrawals</li>
    <li><strong>Deductibility:</strong> If you have no workplace plan, fully deductible at any income</li>
    <li><strong>Required distributions:</strong> Must begin at age 73</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Roth IRA</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Tax benefit:</strong> No deduction now, tax-free withdrawals in retirement</li>
    <li><strong>Income limits:</strong> Phase-out starts at $146,000 (single) / $230,000 (married)</li>
    <li><strong>Flexibility:</strong> Contributions (not earnings) can be withdrawn anytime tax-free</li>
    <li><strong>No required distributions:</strong> Can let it grow for life</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Backdoor Roth</h3>
  <p>
    If your income exceeds Roth IRA limits, you can use the "backdoor" strategy:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Contribute to a non-deductible Traditional IRA</li>
    <li>Convert to Roth IRA</li>
    <li>Pay taxes only on any gains (minimal if done quickly)</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This is legal but requires proper documentation. Consult a tax professional if you have existing
    Traditional IRA balances (pro-rata rule applies).
  </p>

