  <h3>Quick Decision Guide</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Income under $50,000:</strong> Start with Traditional or Roth IRA</li>
    <li><strong>Income $50,000-150,000:</strong> SEP IRA for simplicity, Solo 401(k) for maximum savings</li>
    <li><strong>Income over $150,000:</strong> Solo 401(k) to maximize the $69,000 limit</li>
    <li><strong>Have employees:</strong> SEP (simple) or SIMPLE IRA (employee participation)</li>
    <li><strong>Want Roth option:</strong> Solo 401(k) with Roth feature or separate Roth IRA</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- How to Open -->
  <h2 id="how-to-open">How to Open Each Account Type</h2>

  <h3>Opening a Solo 401(k)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose a provider (Fidelity, Schwab, Vanguard recommended)</li>
    <li>Complete plan adoption agreement (online, 15-30 minutes)</li>
    <li>Provide EIN (or SSN for sole proprietors)</li>
    <li>Fund the account via transfer or check</li>
    <li>Choose investments (target date funds are simple starting point)</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Deadline:</strong> Plan must be established by December 31 of the tax year for which you want
    to make employee contributions.
  </p>

  <h3>Opening a SEP IRA</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose a provider with SEP IRA option</li>
    <li>Complete IRS Form 5305-SEP (usually done through provider)</li>
    <li>Open the SEP IRA account</li>
    <li>Fund and invest</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Deadline:</strong> Can be opened and funded up to tax filing deadline including extensions.
  </p>

  <h3>Opening a Traditional or Roth IRA</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Choose any brokerage (Fidelity, Schwab, Vanguard, etc.)</li>
    <li>Open account online (10 minutes)</li>
    <li>Link bank account</li>
    <li>Fund and invest</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Deadline:</strong> April 15 (tax filing deadline, no extensions) for prior year contributions.
  </p>

  <!-- Automation -->
  <h2 id="automation">Automation Strategies</h2>
  <p>
    The best retirement plan is one you actually follow. Automation removes willpower from the equation.
  </p>

  <h3>Percentage of Income</h3>
  <p>
    Commit to saving a percentage of every payment:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Minimum:</strong> 10-15% of gross income</li>
    <li><strong>Ideal:</strong> 20-25% if you started late or want to retire early</li>
    <li>Transfer the percentage immediately when paid, before spending</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Automated Transfers</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Set up automatic monthly transfers from checking to retirement account</li>
    <li>If income varies, use a conservative base amount and top up manually</li>
    <li>Some brokerages allow percentage-based or recurring transfers</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Pay Yourself First</h3>
  <p>
    Structure your finances so retirement contributions happen first:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Client pays you</li>
    <li>Retirement contribution transfers automatically</li>
    <li>Taxes set aside (25-30%)</li>
    <li>Remaining goes to operating/personal accounts</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Quarterly True-Up</h3>
  <p>
    Review contributions quarterly:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Calculate year-to-date income</li>
    <li>Project full-year income</li>
    <li>Adjust contributions to maximize limits without over-contributing</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Conclusion: Start Today</h2>
  <p>
    The best time to start saving for retirement was years ago. The second best time is today.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Your action plan:</strong>
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Choose an account type based on your income (Solo 401(k) for most earning $50k+)</li>
    <li>Open an account this week (Fidelity, Schwab, or Vanguard—all free)</li>
    <li>Set up automatic contributions (start with what's comfortable)</li>
    <li>Increase contributions when income grows</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Your future self is counting on the decisions you make today. Don't let the freedom of freelancing
    become a liability in retirement—let it become the vehicle that builds real wealth.
  </p>

